    imageAlt: "A real estate agent taking a call at her desk beside a laptop",
    html: "<h2>The short answer</h2><p>You <strong>can</strong> click send on a newsletter from a personal Gmail address. Whether it lands in the inbox is a different question, and the honest answer is: increasingly, no. A consumer <strong>@gmail.com</strong> account was built for one-to-one email, not for pushing a monthly market update to a few hundred past clients and leads at once. The moment you use it like a broadcast tool, you run into sending caps, authentication problems, and spam filtering that quietly buries your message.</p><p>If your newsletter is part of how you stay top-of-mind, sending it from Gmail is working against you. Here is exactly why, and what a professional setup looks like instead.</p><h2>Why Gmail was never meant for newsletters</h2><p>A free Gmail account is a personal mailbox. It comes with daily sending limits measured in the low hundreds of recipients, and those limits exist specifically to stop the account from being used as a bulk sender. When you blast a newsletter to your whole database, you either hit that ceiling or you trip the exact behavior the limit was designed to catch.</p><p>There is also the address itself. When your newsletter arrives from <strong>yourname@gmail.com</strong>, it tells every recipient and every spam filter the same thing: this is a person emailing from a free consumer account, not a business communicating from its own domain. For a real estate professional whose entire pitch is credibility and local authority, that mismatch does real damage before anyone reads a word.</p><h3>What changed with mailbox providers</h3><p>The bigger shift is on the receiving side. The major mailbox providers, Gmail and Yahoo included, tightened their rules for anyone sending mail in volume. To reliably reach the inbox now, a sender needs to prove who they are through domain authentication, honor a one-click unsubscribe, and keep spam complaints low. Those rules assume you control a sending domain. You cannot properly authenticate mail \"from\" gmail.com because you do not own gmail.com. That single fact is why sending a newsletter from a free Gmail address has quietly stopped working the way it used to.</p><h2>What actually breaks when you send from @gmail.com</h2><p>The failures are rarely obvious, which is what makes them dangerous. You hit send, see no bounce, and assume it worked.</p><ul><li><strong>Silent spam placement.</strong> Your newsletter is delivered, but to the Promotions tab or the spam folder. Open rates crater and you never know why.</li><li><strong>Sending caps.</strong> A large send gets throttled or cut off partway through, so half your list never receives it.</li><li><strong>Failed authentication.</strong> Because the mail cannot be verified against a domain you own, cautious inbox providers deprioritize or reject it.</li><li><strong>Reputation you cannot control.</strong> A shared consumer service means your standing is tied to strangers' behavior, not just your own.</li><li><strong>No real reporting.</strong> You get none of the deliverability signals a business needs to see whether the message actually landed.</li></ul><p>These are not settings you can toggle off inside Gmail; they are structural. We cover the full picture in our <a href=\"/solutions\">guide to real estate email deliverability</a>, and the fix is always the same: stop borrowing someone else's mailbox and start sending from infrastructure you own.</p><h2>What sending a newsletter properly requires</h2><p>Getting a newsletter into the inbox reliably is not about a fancier design or a bigger list. It comes down to a few unglamorous pieces of plumbing that have to be in place before the first send.</p><h3>Your own domain and authentication</h3><p>Real email deliverability starts with a business domain, for example <strong>you@yourbrokerage.com</strong>, and the three authentication records that sit behind it: SPF, DKIM, and DMARC. In plain terms, these prove to Gmail and Yahoo that the mail genuinely came from you and was not forged. You can only set these up on a domain you control, which is the entire reason a <strong>@gmail.com</strong> address is a dead end for anything sent at scale.</p><h3>A proper mailbox and sending path</h3><p>On a managed <strong>Microsoft 365</strong> foundation, your domain, Outlook mailbox, and authentication are configured together so business mail is signed and verifiable from day one. That is the difference between an address that looks professional and one that is actually trusted by the systems deciding whether you reach the inbox. You can see how that fits with the rest of the tooling in <a href=\"/solutions\">what a managed foundation includes</a>.</p><h3>A real list and unsubscribe discipline</h3><p>Volume sending also means honoring unsubscribes cleanly, keeping your list to people who expect to hear from you, and watching complaint rates. Consumer Gmail gives you none of the tooling to do this properly. A managed CRM and email layer does, because it is built for exactly this job.</p><h2>What to do instead</h2><p>If you are running your business on a free Gmail account today, you do not need to become an email-infrastructure expert overnight. You need the foundation set up correctly once and maintained so it keeps working. That means a business domain, a real Outlook mailbox, authentication records configured properly, and a sending path built for newsletters and campaigns rather than one-to-one replies.</p><p>Done right, this also unlocks everything downstream: consistent branding across every message, automated follow-up that does not bounce, and reporting that tells you what actually reached your database. When you are ready to size it for your team, our <a href=\"/pricing\">plans and seats</a> lay out the options, and you can <a href=\"/contact\">book a consult</a> to have it provisioned for you.</p><p>So, can you still send a real estate newsletter from a Gmail address? For a quick note to one client, sure. For the recurring, list-wide communication your brand depends on, it is time to move to infrastructure you own and control.</p><h2>Get the foundation set up for you</h2><p>Genesis AI provisions and manages complete AI infrastructure for real estate professionals, a Microsoft 365 foundation, AI workflows and automation, and an optional Genesis CRM layer, fully managed month over month. If you want your newsletter, your mailbox, and your follow-up all sending from a domain that actually reaches the inbox, <strong>book a consult at geai.us</strong>.</p>",
