    readTime: "6 min read",
    image: "/images/blog/real-estate-emails-going-to-spam.webp",
    imageAlt: "A real estate professional consulting with a client across a desk",
    html: "<p>If your listing alerts, open-house invites, and follow-ups keep landing in the junk folder, the problem usually isn't what you wrote — it's how your email is set up to be sent and trusted. Inbox providers like Gmail and Outlook decide placement based on your <strong>sender reputation</strong> and whether your domain is properly authenticated, long before anyone reads your subject line.</p><h2>Why do real estate emails go to spam?</h2><p>Real estate emails go to spam for four repeatable reasons: your sending domain isn't authenticated, you're sending from a free consumer address, your content trips filter rules, or you're mailing contacts who never open. Industry guidance is consistent that missing authentication — SPF, DKIM, and DMARC — and a poor sender reputation are the biggest drivers, not the occasional \"spammy\" word.</p><p>The frustrating part for most agents is that the message feels personal and legitimate — a market update to past clients, a new listing to your sphere — yet it still gets filtered. That's because the filter never judges intent. It judges signals, and consumer-grade setups send weak ones.</p><h2>Your domain isn't authenticated</h2><p>This is the single most common cause. Email authentication is a set of DNS records that prove a message genuinely came from you and wasn't spoofed. Deliverability specialists widely note that a sudden drop in inbox placement usually traces back to lapsed or missing authentication.</p><h3>The three records that matter</h3><ul><li><strong>SPF</strong> tells receiving servers which systems are allowed to send email for your domain.</li><li><strong>DKIM</strong> adds a cryptographic signature so the receiver can verify the message wasn't altered in transit.</li><li><strong>DMARC</strong> ties the two together and tells inbox providers what to do when a message fails — and gives you reporting on who's sending as you.</li></ul><p>Since 2024, Google and Yahoo have tightened requirements for anyone sending in volume, effectively making these records table stakes rather than nice-to-haves. If you're running blasts through a CRM or marketing tool without all three configured on your own domain, filtering is the expected outcome, not bad luck. This is exactly the kind of thing our <a href=\"/solutions\">complete guide to real estate email deliverability</a> walks through end to end.</p><h2>You're sending from a free consumer address</h2><p>If your \"from\" address ends in gmail.com, yahoo.com, or a provider you don't control, you can't authenticate it as a business sender — and providers increasingly penalize bulk mail from consumer domains. Sending marketing volume through an inbox built for one-to-one personal mail is a mismatch the filters are designed to catch.</p><p>A professional setup means sending from your own domain — you@yourbrokerage.com — on a business email platform like Microsoft 365 where SPF, DKIM, and DMARC can actually be applied. That single change moves you from \"unverified stranger\" to \"known sender\" in the eyes of the inbox.</p><h2>Your content is tripping filters</h2><p>Content matters less than reputation, but it still counts — especially once your reputation is already shaky. A few patterns reliably raise flags in real estate mail.</p><h3>Common triggers to clean up</h3><ul><li><strong>Salesy trigger words.</strong> Terms like \"free,\" \"cash,\" \"refinance,\" and \"opportunity\" in subject lines are flagged more often, per longstanding email marketing guidance.</li><li><strong>Image-heavy messages.</strong> A common best practice is roughly a 60/40 text-to-image ratio; image-only emails and broken or shortened links are classic spam signals.</li><li><strong>No plain-text version and no clear unsubscribe.</strong> Marketing email in the U.S. is governed by CAN-SPAM, which requires a real physical address and a working opt-out. Missing those hurts both compliance and placement.</li></ul><h2>You're emailing people who don't engage</h2><p>Opt-in alone doesn't guarantee the inbox. Providers watch engagement — opens, replies, and whether recipients fish you out of spam — and a list full of dead addresses drags your whole domain down. Mailing an old, unpruned database is one of the fastest ways to teach Gmail that your mail is unwanted.</p><p>Practically, that means removing hard bounces, suppressing contacts who haven't engaged in months, and prioritizing the segments that actually open. A smaller list that responds beats a huge list that ignores you every time.</p><h2>How to fix it, in order</h2><ol><li><strong>Move to a business email platform</strong> on your own domain instead of a consumer inbox.</li><li><strong>Configure SPF, DKIM, and DMARC</strong> on that domain — and confirm every tool that sends on your behalf is included.</li><li><strong>Warm up gradually</strong> if the domain is new, so you build reputation instead of triggering a volume spike.</li><li><strong>Clean the list</strong> and keep sending consistently to engaged contacts.</li><li><strong>Tidy the content</strong> — real subject lines, balanced text, working links, a clear unsubscribe and mailing address.</li></ol><p>None of this is exotic, but it lives in DNS records, admin consoles, and ongoing monitoring — the plumbing most agents never signed up to manage. When it's set up once and left alone, records drift, tools get added, and deliverability quietly erodes again. That's the difference between buying another app and standing up a managed foundation; you can see <a href=\"/solutions\">what a managed foundation includes</a> and how the pieces connect rather than pile up.</p><h2>Stop guessing at the inbox</h2><p>Deliverability isn't a one-time fix — it's infrastructure that needs an owner. If you'd rather have authentication, your business email, and your sending tools configured correctly and watched over time, that's the work we do. Compare <a href=\"/pricing\">plans and seats</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">book a consult</a> to talk through your current setup.</p><p>Genesis AI provisions and manages complete AI infrastructure for real estate professionals — a Microsoft 365 foundation, AI workflows and automation, and an optional Genesis CRM layer, fully managed month over month. Book a consult at <strong>geai.us</strong>.</p>",
